      "writtenOn": "2021-01-22T13:09:46Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "How safe is it to call a random hook in NMI context? I don\u0027t think this can be made to work in the general case.",
      "revId": "145036233d366d60fa4ba639f5e14c58a900f63c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "bfe5f79e_4764b612",
        "filename": "arch/arm64/kernel/traps.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 770,
      "author": {
        "id": 1743420
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-01-25T11:55:37Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I understand worrying about some behaviors within NMI context.\nFor two reasons, I don\u0027t think our hook is so dangerous and I think it is similar with other panic_notifiers.\n\n(1) Fortunately, it never escape from the NMI context hear and does not return to the normal kernel context, because it will fall into panic().\n(2) When panic() is called, other panic_notifiers are also performed in the same NMI context.",
      "parentUuid": "55247ccd_fde1bdb3",
      "revId": "145036233d366d60fa4ba639f5e14c58a900f63c",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
